---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Setup, users, teams, and AI — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-sky">For Platform admins</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Setup, users, teams, and AI</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <h2>Empty instance Setup</h2>
            <p>
                On first visit with an empty user table, complete Setup to create the
                initial Platform admin. That user is placed on the seeded system team and
                system project (Team admin + project Admin). Optional “Team name” on Setup
                renames the system team's display name in the same transaction; the code
                <code class="text-foreground">default</code> does not change and the system
                team/project cannot be deleted. Later teams do not auto-seed a project.
            </p>
            <p>
                If the system team is still named Default after login, a dismissible banner
                can link to team settings. It is not a blocking wizard.
            </p>

            <h2>Users versus invite</h2>
            <p>
                Create user with a temporary password remains a bootstrap path you own.
                Invite is the human path: creates pending login + memberships, email token,
                accept is password-only. You may invite to one or more teams in a single
                invite, each with its own Team admin flag and project roles. Only you may
                grant Platform admin. Cannot remove or demote the last enabled Platform admin.
            </p>
            <p>
                Identity Developer is retired. Users UI is Platform admin versus standard
                user. Forged team/project cookies are ignored. Authorization is enforced
                in the UI and on the API.
            </p>

            <h2>Teams</h2>
            <p>
                You create and archive teams. Optional first project fields on the create
                modal run in the same transaction. Left blank, land on Administration with
                an empty team. You may create a project in any team. You may act in any
                team or project without membership, but the UI still picks a current
                project — there is no “view all projects on one list” in v1.
            </p>

            <h2>AI configuration</h2>
            <p>
                Instance AI settings live in Administration: chat LLM, SQL-generation LLM,
                and embeddings. SQL Assist remains draft-only regardless of provider.
                Embeddings live on PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector (fixed dimension; mismatch
                fails closed). Schema-source fetch strings are never used to execute SQL.
                Structured logging and OpenTelemetry must never include full connection
                strings or decrypted secrets. Developer walkthrough:
                <a href="/docs/developer-sql-assist" class="text-link hover:underline">SQL Assist</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>Break-glass</h2>
            <p>
                You still pick a current project in the footer. Superuser does not mean a
                merged catalog. API keys never inherit this role. Worker and scheduler
                hosts are trusted control-plane processes and are not user sessions.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
